        "information": "Source: https://www.instagram.com/popular/the-damingos-band-interview-1970s-newspaper/\nTitle: The Damingos Band Interview 1970s Newspaper\nChunks: 13,14\n# The Damingos Band Interview 1970s Newspaper\n## 1970s band\n[[](https://www.instagram.com/70sheavyrockfan) 70sheavyrockfan 193K The band was often called \"America's answer to Black Sabbath\"! Amazed to watch this footage with no sound on youtube by this band from the 70s @bang_the_band. A totally gem. If you love Black Sabbath and the roots of heavy metal check out their first albums! Authentic Hard Heavy Rock from the 70s. Don't forget to follow us @ancientgreaserecords #blacksabbath #hardrock #rockmusic #rocknroll #ledzepoelin #guitarplayer #rock #fuzz #guitarplayer #drums #vintage #vintagevideo #rockband #rock #electricguitar](https://www.instagram.com/reel/DC95wjmpPM9/?utm_source=popular_topic_grid)\n[[](https://www.instagram.com/70sdesperado) 70sdesperado](https://www.instagram.com/reel/DIb1MQIxvj3/?utm_source=popular_topic_grid)\n[[](https://www.instagram.com/irish_alternative_music) irish_alterna... 82.3K A bit of everything in this video lol! No goths this time possibly too nonchalant to feature in this interview! The format seems to be some sort of no holds barred massive group interview where whoever speaks the loudest gets the mic lol! I’ve never heard the word “agro” used so much I feel like all these music subcultures have amazing people who love the fashion and sound of the music alone however the interviewer seems more interested in who is gonna cause trouble first who lol typical 1980s rté mindset! #irishalternative #ireland1980s #rte](https://www.instagram.com/reel/DNqjGRVqwoq/?utm_source=popular_topic_grid)\n\n# The Damingos Band Interview 1970s Newspaper\n## 1970s band\n[[](https://www.instagram.com/adrianyounge) adrianyounge 19.4K @osmutantesbanda playing “Panis Et Circenses” Bread and Circus from Tropicalia ou Panis ‘68. #musicabrasiliana #psych #live #brasil](https://www.instagram.com/reel/DFLZAfGS047/?utm_source=popular_topic_grid)\n[[](https://www.instagram.com/detroitbandsthatsuck) detroitbandst... 12K Bob Seger in 1970 was still early in the climb, long before the beard, the mythology, and the version of him most people remember. Backed by The Bob Seger System, “Ramblin’ Gamblin’ Man” comes from a moment when Seger was focused on breaking through, not summing anything up. There is no reflection here, only forward motion and volume. This is Detroit rock at its most direct. The song runs on a tense, galloping rhythm that pulls from garage rock and Midwest blues, pushed by guitars left rough around the edges and an organ that cuts straight through the mix. Seger’s voice already had grit, but in 1970 it sounded sharper and more impatient, built to carry over loud rooms rather than linger on meaning. The Bob Seger System mattered because it functioned like a real working band, giving the music its weight and urgency. “Ramblin’ Gamblin’ Man” was about impact more than polish, the kind of performance meant to take over a Detroit crowd fast and keep it there. This period gets buried under the later legend, but it shows Seger before the image settled in, pre beard and pre nostalgia, rocking hard and loud in the way Detroit always demanded.](https://www.instagram.com/reel/DT0wlfIgW1z/?utm_source=popular_topic_grid)\nGet the full experience\nSign up for Instagram\nMeta\nAbout\nBlog\nJobs\nHelp\nAPI\nPrivacy\nTerms\nLocations\nPopular\nInstagram Lite\nMeta AI\nMuse\nThreads\nContact Uploading & Non-Users\nMeta Verified\nEnglish\n© 2026 Instagram from Meta\n\n",
