          "0": "# Rhubarb Ad 1970s Most Stylish Produce\nLog In\nSign Up\nWatch reels about rhubarb ad 1970s most stylish produce from people around the world.\n[[](https://www.instagram.com/themadplantman) themadplantman 195 The Tale of the Smug Victorian Rhubarb Victorians pampered rhubarb in the dark so it’d grow long, pale, and posh — you could almost hear it creak! Ever tried forcing rhubarb, my ’ansome? #GardenHistory #Rhubarb #VictorianGarden #ForcingSheds #KitchenGarden #AllotmentUK #GrowYourOwn #SoilToSupper #CornishGardener #TheMadPlantMan #GranferTrebillcock](https://www.instagram.com/reel/DV50t-ZgIFf/?utm_source=popular_topic_grid)\n[[](https://www.instagram.com/fiveminutegardener) fiveminutegar... 1,236 Want the sweetest rhubarb you’ll ever taste? The secret is forcing it in the dark Forced rhubarb is grown away from light by covering the crown with a pot or container… I think I need a bigger pot next time The darkness produces tender pink stalks that are sweeter and less acidic than normal rhubarb. It’s one of the simplest tricks in gardening and it completely transforms the flavour. Try forcing one of your rhubarb crowns and taste the difference 🌱 What are your favourite rhubarb recipes? I’m thinking of a jam or maybe trying a cinnamon bun recipe from @bakesbymichelle that has rhubarb in it #rhubarb #gardeninghack #growyourown #gardening #gardeningtips](https://www.instagram.com/reel/DWJFVwQDIHW/?utm_source=popular_topic_grid)",
          "1": "# Rhubarb Ad 1970s Most Stylish Produce\n[[](https://www.instagram.com/rachelallencooks) rachelallencooks 62K There’s something quietly satisfying about forced rhubarb — one of my favourite early signs that the season is turning. Cover the crown with a terracotta pot, block out the light, and the plant does the rest. The result is long, tender, blush-pink stalks that are sweeter and more delicate than anything grown in the open air. 🌸 It works best from late winter once the crown has had a proper cold spell — the frost is what triggers it into growth. Leave it for four to six weeks and you’ll find pale, jewel-like stalks reaching upward. 🧑‍🍳 In the kitchen, forced rhubarb needs a little less sugar than you might think, and very little time — a classic crumble, a fool with softly whipped cream, or simply stewed and spooned over porridge in the morning. One thing to keep in mind: don’t force the same crown two years running. Give it a season to recover and it will keep going for years. 🌱 Are you growing rhubarb this year?](https://www.instagram.com/reel/DVmgxKljDc1/?utm_source=popular_topic_grid)",
          "2": "# Rhubarb Ad 1970s Most Stylish Produce\n[[](https://www.instagram.com/gardeningwithben) gardeningwithben 548 🌱 That exciting moment when the rhubarb really starts growing! 👨‍🌾 There’s nothing better than walking down the allotment and seeing the rhubarb bursting into life again. Those big green leaves and thick red stalks pushing up through the soil can only mean one thing… the first harvest of the season is getting closer! After a long winter, it’s one of the first crops that reminds you the growing season has truly begun. I don’t know about you, but I’m already dreaming about that first crumble or rhubarb dessert from the garden. 🍰 Who else gets excited when the rhubarb starts growing again? Have you got some growing in your garden or allotment this year? Keep smiling, keep shining, and keep gardening with Ben 🌿 #gardeningwithben #rhubarb #allotmentlife #growyourownfood #allotmentgarden gardeninguk vegetablegarden growyourown gardenharvest ukgardener](https://www.instagram.com/reel/DVb4okKDFS5/?utm_source=popular_topic_grid)\n[[](https://www.instagram.com/tinyedenuk) tinyedenuk 179 Rhubarb: Delicious and Dramatic in a cottage garden 🌿 The first signs of my Timperley rhubarb emerging and I couldn’t be more excited 🌱❤️ Rhubarb is one of my absolute favourites in the garden. Not just for the delicious harvest to come, but also for how beautiful it is. Those vibrant red stems pushing up through the soil and the huge, exotic-looking leaves unfolding always feel like a real sign that the garden is waking up again. In a cottage garden it adds that drama and vibrant colour. Edible, ornamental, and full of promise for the season ahead. #tinyedenuk #rubarb #ediblegarden #growfood #kitchengarden](https://www.instagram.com/reel/DV6LD4BjTjo/?utm_source=popular_topic_grid)",
          "3": "# Rhubarb Ad 1970s Most Stylish Produce\n[[](https://www.instagram.com/diaryofayorkshiregardener) diaryofayorks... 180K It’s exactly a month on and I didn’t expect it to be this big! 😱 It was covered in ants but they all disappeared within minutes of uncovering. I snapped a good chunk of them off to take home for a crumble 😍 All you need is something to exclude the light (I use an old bin weighed down with bricks). The darkness encourages long, tender and sweeter stems that can grow much earlier than usual. Last year was my first year trying it and it’s now something I’ll look forward to doing every year! It’s nice to harvest something again 🥰 Have you ever forced rhubarb before? ☺️ . . . #rhubarb #forcedrhubarb #allotment #homegrownfood #gardeningtips](https://www.instagram.com/reel/DWI-bRMADNI/?utm_source=popular_topic_grid)\n[[](https://www.instagram.com/rooted__athome) rooted__athome 2,293 Have you tried forcing your rhubarb? If you have a rhubarb crown you can force the buds by excluding light to encourage early shoots. Crowns less than 3 years old benefit from a gradual build up to harvest over the years to allow it to establish so check how old your plant is first. Only force after the plant has had a proper winter chill. Cover the emerging buds with a large pot or darkened cloche. I am using a large flower pot that I had available. Check on your rhubarb every week and harvest when they have reached the top of your vessel. This first harvest is sweet and tender. Very satisfying to be able to harvest some produce from the kitchen garden in the less productive garden months. Have you forced yours yet?](https://www.instagram.com/reel/DVN9q8MiIDC/?utm_source=popular_topic_grid)",
          "4": "# Rhubarb Ad 1970s Most Stylish Produce\n[[](https://www.instagram.com/indigenousfarms) indigenousfarms 350 We’ve been working on a new crop that most people know, but have never seen like this. Introducing….Forced Rhubarb. Dormant crowns are dug from the field and brought indoors to grow in complete darkness and harvested under candlelight. 🕯️ The result? Tender, sweet stalks with electric color and a flavor profile you’ll never get from field grown rhubarb. This ancient technique is rooted in Old English market gardening, where forced rhubarb became an early season delicacy. After my first bite of a tender pink stalk pulled from the darkness, I knew we were onto something special.](https://www.instagram.com/reel/DVZ0Spvj4vT/?utm_source=popular_topic_grid)\n[[](https://www.instagram.com/helenlikesplants) helenlikesplants 23.2K If you’ve got an allotment, you probably inherited a rhubarb plant…but if you didn’t , here’s how to start one (& a few things to know in general!) 🩷 #rhubarb #vegpatch #allotment #allotmenttips #gardeningtips](https://www.instagram.com/reel/DVIbar3iDE2/?utm_source=popular_topic_grid)\n[[](https://www.instagram.com/mapperton_estate) mapperton_estate 5,359 Who knew rhubarb could cause this much excitement? 🍃 The kitchen garden team were beyond excited pulling up the first rhubarb of the season. Bright pink stems, fresh from the soil and perfect for a classic rhubarb crumble. What could be better? What are your favourite rhubarb recipes? Let us know in the comments 💭 #mapperton #kitchengarden #rhubarb #growyourown #seasonalproduce](https://www.instagram.com/reel/DV_xl51CUbu/?utm_source=popular_topic_grid)",
          "5": "# Rhubarb Ad 1970s Most Stylish Produce\n[[](https://www.instagram.com/allotment_with_vilija) allotment_wit... 145 #rhubarb is rhubarbing 🥳 I tried to grow it for the first two seasons but had no success! Then a lovely plot neighbour offered us some rhubarb crowns, which we were super grateful for. Now it’s the second season and they are doing amazingly 🥺♥️🌸✨ Thank you to Bob & Sue x #allotment #allotmentuk #growyourown #gardening 🌱 @Gintaras Murauskas ♥️](https://www.instagram.com/reel/DVj1EzuCBgn/?utm_source=popular_topic_grid)\n[[](https://www.instagram.com/plotandpetals) plotandpetals 910 First harvest of the season: forced rhubarb straight from the darkness of an old chimney pot! Grown without light for tender, blush-pink stalks and a lovely sweet and tart flavour. Delicious! #RhubarbSeason #ForcedRhubarb #GardenHarvest #GrowYourOwn #SeasonalEating](https://www.instagram.com/reel/DV_q0E3Mk_0/?utm_source=popular_topic_grid)\nGet the full experience\nSign up for Instagram\nMeta\nAbout\nBlog\nJobs\nHelp\nAPI\nPrivacy\nTerms\nLocations\nPopular\nInstagram Lite\nMeta AI\nMuse\nThreads\nContact Uploading & Non-Users\nMeta Verified\nEnglish\n© 2026 Instagram from Meta"
