        "texts": {
          "4": "Rhubarb growing and the rhubarb trains are featured in Wakefield Museum. In 2005, Wakefield council erected a sculpture depicting a rhubarb plant in Holmfield Park Wakefield.[[16]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhubarb_Triangle#cite_note-16) In 2016, the Hepworth Wakefield exhibited photographs by Martin Parr, including a series about the Rhubarb Triangle.[[17]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhubarb_Triangle#cite_note-17)\nIn Frances Brody's 2019 novel _The Body on the Train_ (Piatkus: ISBN978-0-349-42306-7), a murdered man's body is found on a rhubarb train from Ardsley, on its arrival at [King's Cross](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London_King's_Cross_railway_station \"London King's Cross railway station\"). The book's cover illustration features boxes of rhubarb on a platform beside a train.\n*   Rhubarb forcer\n**Notes**\n**Citations**",
          "5": "1.   Jump up to: 12Markham 2005, p.118  \n2.   Jump up to: 12_EU name status_, BBC, 25 February 2010, retrieved 25 February 2010  \n3.   Jump up to: 123_Working Lunch_, BBC, 7 February 2006, retrieved 21 February 2010  \n4.   Jump up to: 12_Map of the Week_, BBC, retrieved 4 March 2010  \n5.   Jump up to: 12_Forced rhubarb_, Brandy Carr Nurseries, archived from the original on 9 March 2010, retrieved 21 February 2010  \n6.   ↑Mackay, Mairi (19 February 2008), _Rhubarb_, Times Online, archived from the original on 16 February 2007, retrieved 21 February 2010  \n7.   Jump up to: 12Jack, Ian (18 January 2008), \"Food and Drink\", _The Guardian_, retrieved 21 February 2010  \n8.   ↑_A Shoddy Tale_(PDF), Watsonia.org, p.2, archived from the original(PDF) on 11 November 2009, retrieved 21 February 2010  \n9.   Jump up to: 12_Rhubarb Triangle_, Morley Archives, 17 August 2014, retrieved 26 February 2022  \n10.   ↑\"Rhubarb Triangle\"((Subscription or UK public library membership required)), _Oxford Oxford English Dictionary Third Edition_, retrieved 10 November 2010  \n11.   ↑Robinson 1965, p.128  \n12.   ↑_Application to register Yorkshire Forced Rhubarb_(PDF), DEFRA, archived from the original(PDF) on 22 August 2013, retrieved 25 February 2010  ",
          "6": "13.   Jump up to: 12_Yorkshire rhubarb joins Europe's protected food elite_, DEFRA, archived from the original on 22 August 2013, retrieved 17 April 2016  \n14.   ↑Bates, Stephen (25 February 2010), \"European Protection\", _The Guardian_, retrieved 25 February 2010  \n15.   ↑_Rhubarb Festival_, Wakefield Council, archived from the original on 5 February 2010, retrieved 21 February 2010  \n16.   ↑Bell 2009, p.28  \n17.   ↑\"The Rhubarb Triangle And Other Stories: Photographs By Martin Parr\". _The Hepworth Wakefield_. Retrieved 9 January 2024.\n**Bibliography**\n*   Bell, Richard (2009), _Walks in the Rhubarb Triangle_, Willow Island Editions, ISBN978-1-902467-18-4  \n*   Markham, Len (2005), _The Wharncliffe Companion to Wakefield & District_, Wharncliffe, ISBN1-903425-89-1  \n*   Robinson, Harry (1965), _Geography for business studies_, MacDonald & Evans\n\n*   Rhubarb Sheds; images from Leodis  \n*   E. Oldroyd & Sons Ltd  \n*   Asquith GrowersArchived 24 February 2010 at the Wayback Machine  \n*   Yorkshire Film Archive (documentary): The Rhubarb Triangle (2000)Archived 9 December 2018 at the Wayback Machine\n53°44′N 1°30′W / 53.73°N 1.5°W"
